{{also|Welch}}
==English==
The origin of the term "welch" comes from "Welsh," the nationality, and is racist in origin.  The Welsh people were widely discriminated against, being thought of as unreliable and dishonest, and so "to Welsh" became synonymous with reneging on payments owed or agreements made.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|es}}

# A person who defaults on an obligation, especially a small one.
#: ''She's a '''welch.'''  That isn't hers, I lent her that watering can three years ago.''

===Verb===
{{en-verb|welches|welching|welched}}

# To [[fail]] to [[repay]] a small debt.

====Derived terms====
* [[welcher]] ''one who repeatedly is a '''welch'''.''

